def lcs_of_three(str1, str2, str3, n1, n2, n3):
    # Initialize the 3D DP table
    dp = [[[0 for _ in range(n3+1)] for _ in range(n2+1)] for _ in range(n1+1)]

    # Fill the DP table
    for i in range(1, n1+1):
        for j in range(1, n2+1):
            for k in range(1, n3+1):
                if str1[i-1] == str2[j-1] == str3[k-1]:
                    dp[i][j][k] = 1 + dp[i-1][j-1][k-1]
                else:
                    dp[i][j][k] = max(dp[i-1][j][k], dp[i][j-1][k], dp[i][j][k-1])

    return dp[n1][n2][n3]
```
The `lcs_of_three` function initializes a 3D DP table where `dp[i][j][k]` represents the length of the longest common subsequence of the first `i` characters of `str1`, the first `j` characters of `str2`, and the first `k` characters of `str3`. The function iterates through each character of `str1`, `str2`, and `str3`, updating the DP table based on whether the characters match. Finally, it returns the value stored at `dp[n1][n2][n3]`, which represents the length of the longest common subsequence of the entire three strings.

